{
  "concept": "The area of any oblique triangle equals one-half the base times the perpendicular height from the opposite vertex, and that height is the leg of a right triangle giving h = c·sin of the angle adjacent to the perpendicular — whether the foot lands on the base (acute) or on its extension (obtuse).",
  "figureType": "diagram",
  "elements": [
    "horizontal base segment labeled b, left endpoint vertex α, right endpoint vertex γ",
    "left side labeled c from α up to apex β",
    "right side labeled a sloping from β down to γ",
    "vertex labels α, β, γ",
    "side labels a, b, c",
    "dashed vertical altitude labeled h from β to its foot on the base line",
    "small square right-angle marker at the foot of h",
    "interior angle arc at α labeled α",
    "dashed leftward extension of base b beyond α (visible only in obtuse arrangement)",
    "exterior angle arc at α between side c and the extension, labeled α′ (visible only in obtuse arrangement)",
    "annotation text 'Area = ½ · b · c · sin α' revealed with the altitude"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "apexPosition",
      "type": "slider",
      "label": "Apex β horizontal position",
      "range": [-3, 4, 0.1],
      "default": 1.5,
      "teaches": "Dragging β horizontally across α slides the altitude foot from inside the base onto the dashed extension, and the angle that supplies the height switches from the interior α to its supplement α′ — the perpendicular construction itself never changes, only where it lands."
    },
    {
      "id": "caseView",
      "type": "toggle",
      "label": "Angle at α",
      "options": ["Acute at α", "Obtuse at α"],
      "default": "Acute at α",
      "teaches": "The two named cases differ only in whether the perpendicular from β meets the segment b or its extension; in both, h = c·sin of the angle adjacent to the perpendicular, so the same area formula applies."
    },
    {
      "id": "altitudeState",
      "type": "button",
      "label": "Construct altitude h (click base b)",
      "states": ["hidden", "shown"],
      "default": "hidden",
      "teaches": "Dropping the perpendicular converts the oblique triangle into a right triangle whose vertical leg h is exactly the height used in Area = ½·b·h, linking the slanted side c and the angle to the area via h = c·sin α."
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "An oblique triangle",
      "narration": "Base b runs from α to γ with apex β above; sides a and c carry no right angle, so the usual ½·base·height needs a height we must construct.",
      "state": { "caseView": "Acute at α", "apexPosition": 1.5, "altitudeState": "hidden" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Drop the perpendicular",
      "narration": "Clicking the base drops the dashed altitude h from β with a right-angle marker; h is the leg opposite α in a right triangle with hypotenuse c, so h = c·sin α and Area = ½·b·c·sin α.",
      "state": { "caseView": "Acute at α", "apexPosition": 1.5, "altitudeState": "shown" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Obtuse arrangement",
      "narration": "Snapping to the obtuse case moves β left of α; the altitude foot now falls outside the triangle on the dashed base extension, and the relevant angle is the exterior α′ = 180° − α, yet sin α′ = sin α so the formula is unchanged.",
      "state": { "caseView": "Obtuse at α", "apexPosition": -1.2, "altitudeState": "shown" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Sweep the apex",
      "narration": "Drag β continuously across α and watch the foot slide from the interior of b onto the extension while the labeled angle flips from α to α′ — one height construction serves every oblique triangle.",
      "state": { "caseView": "Acute at α", "apexPosition": 0.2, "altitudeState": "shown" }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "Whether the perpendicular from the apex lands on the base or on its extension, the height is h = c·sin of the angle adjacent to that perpendicular, so Area = ½·b·c·sin α holds for acute and obtuse triangles alike.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Render a single morphing oblique triangle on a horizontal base b (α left, γ right) rather than two static panels; the toggle simply snaps the apex to a representative acute (β right of α) or obtuse (β left of α) position. Triangle outline solid black; altitude h and the base extension dashed gray; angle arcs in red with α (and α′ in the obtuse case) labeled in italic serif; right-angle foot shown as a small open square. The α′ arc and the dashed leftward extension appear only when β is left of α (obtuse), driven by the apex position so they stay correct even after free dragging. Reveal the 'Area = ½·b·c·sin α' annotation together with the altitude. Keep vertex and side labels (α, β, γ, a, b, c) fixed relative to their edges as the apex moves."
}

